WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.680
Way back in 2003, the final Matrix film,&nbsp;
The Matrix Revolutions, hit the big screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.480
It's hardly regarded as the strongest installment&nbsp;
in the famed trilogy, and by the time it left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.680
theaters, the Matrix craze had significantly&nbsp;
died down. With the 2020s continuing the trend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.560
of endless reboots and sequels, excitement is&nbsp;
back in the air for The Matrix Resurrections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.840
which will debut December 2021 and presumably&nbsp;
pick up where Revolutions left off.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.080
There's no better time to begin refreshing your&nbsp;
memory on everything The Matrix franchise has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.240
to offer, beginning with the 1999&nbsp;
film that captivated a generation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.560
From deep allegories that carry more&nbsp;
meaning today to innovative filmmaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.440
techniques that changed cinema forever, The&nbsp;
Matrix was by all accounts a game-changer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.560
It presented audiences with philosophical&nbsp;
quandaries in an easy-to-understand way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.720
packed a punch through its unique action&nbsp;
sequences, and told a story that was more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.920
worth the price of admission. Plus, it added some&nbsp;
incredible characters to the pop-culture pantheon.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.600
"If you're killed in the Matrix, you die&nbsp;
here?" "The body cannot live without the mind."

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.720
There's no denying Keanu Reeves' Neo&nbsp;
is the poster child of The Matrix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.840
but one would be remiss not to give Carrie&nbsp;
Anne-Moss' Trinity the spotlight she deserves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.520
She played a big hand in the first film's story,&nbsp;
cementing her legacy through one particular scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.200
that still has fans pausing,&nbsp;
rewinding, and watching again.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.040
As we enter The Matrix universe for the&nbsp;
first time, it's made clear that something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.880
isn't quite right. A green-tinted&nbsp;
dystopia looks like a normal city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.080
but we feel in our bones that the whole setup is&nbsp;
different from the world that we know. We move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.040
to the interior of a building swarmed by cops,&nbsp;
eventually meeting Trinity sitting at a computer.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.200
"I think we can handle one little girl."

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.960
Despite being busted, presumably for a&nbsp;
serious crime, she refuses to go quietly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.160
and delivers arguably the coolest-looking&nbsp;
kick in the history of motion pictures.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.400
Trinity hovering before a dumbfounded&nbsp;
police officer has become the defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.800
image of the character even for those who've&nbsp;
never seen The Matrix. The scene itself has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.840
been parodied to death over the years, most&nbsp;
recently getting an homage in 2021's Space Jam:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.720
A New Legacy. The reason it's so iconic&nbsp;
is because of its use of a bullet-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.760
camera rig. It's a setup that included dozens&nbsp;
of cameras all in operation at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.760
capturing a 360-degree image that appears frozen.&nbsp;
Combine that with a green screen, Carrie-Anne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.440
Moss on cables, and slow-motion aftereffects, and&nbsp;
you've got yourself an unforgettable movie moment.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.080
When viewers watched Trinity leap into the air&nbsp;
for the first time, it was clear that The Matrix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.000
wasn't going to look or feel like any action&nbsp;
movie before it. The 1999 film is a showstopper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.920
from beginning to end, but no other scene captured&nbsp;
that pause-worthy wow factor like Trinity's kick.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.160
The famous Trinity kick is an excellent&nbsp;
example of how The Matrix and its sequels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.080
changed the way Hollywood's general&nbsp;
audiences think about visual effects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.240
It was as if the collective zeitgeist took&nbsp;
the red pill and had their eyes opened to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.200
new way of filming action sequences. The Matrix&nbsp;
took the art of the fight scene to a new level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.280
by bringing in one of the biggest names in&nbsp;
the game to choreograph various sequences.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.280
The Matrix directors Lana and&nbsp;
Lilly Wachowski hired director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.560
Yuen Woo Ping to train the actors for six&nbsp;
months prior to filming, according to Unilad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.200
At the time, focusing on stunt choreography for&nbsp;
that long was unheard of for a Hollywood film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.200
But The Matrix proved that fight scenes&nbsp;
could be more than punching and kicking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.400
With the right people on board, you could create a&nbsp;
visually stunning scene that rivals any car chase.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.200
But The Matrix did far more than merely give&nbsp;
us cooler setpieces. The sci-fi flick altered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.920
the way people think about not just action in&nbsp;
movies, but philosophy. The film was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.720
seamlessly blend the two. Without The Matrix,&nbsp;
we may not have had Inception, Eternal Sunshine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.600
of the Spotless Mind, and District 9, just to&nbsp;
name a few. And of course, we also wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:55.600
have The Matrix Resurrections, which drops in&nbsp;
theaters and on HBO Max on December 22, 2021.

